    },
    {
      "name": "Rules of Engagement",
      "type": "feature",
      "system": {
        "featureForm": "passive",
        "description": "<p>During the Duel, the challenged PC is the only one who can make action rolls. Other PCs can only aid the challenged PC indirectly: They can Help an Ally, initiate group action rolls or Tag Team Rolls, and use any of their features that don't directly affect the challenging adversary.</p><section id=\"secret-BOtPLRBWgz5TXdoa\" class=\"secret\"><p>Where does this conflict take place? How does the environment offer the rest of the party an opportunity to aid their ally without interfering?</p></section>",
        "attribution": {},
        "gmNotes": "",
        "resource": null,
        "actions": {},
        "granter": null
      },
      "_id": "AEM0xmcW99tiqKWq",
      "img": "icons/sundries/documents/document-symbol-lightning-brown.webp",
      "effects": [],
      "folder": null,
      "sort": 0,
      "flags": {},
      "_stats": {
        "compendiumSource": null
      },
      "_key": "!actors.items!mLnGz7Fy9d0ttT9F.AEM0xmcW99tiqKWq"
    },
    {
      "name": "Dirty Tricks",
      "type": "feature",
      "system": {
        "featureForm": "action",
        "description": "<p><strong>Spend a Fear</strong> to have the challenging adversary or one of their allies cheat. A PC can notice their underhanded action with a successful Instinct Reaction Roll and respond in kind.</p><section id=\"secret-LzWyF5jF08rjyP7c\" class=\"secret\"><p>What's off -limits in this conflict? How does the adversary's ally bend the rules without breaking them, or break the rules without getting caught? How can the PCs rebalance the scales?</p></section>",
        "attribution": {},
        "gmNotes": "",
        "resource": null,
